id: k8s.configmap
kind: kubernetes
title: ConfigMap
aliases: configmap, config
tags: configuration
questions:
  - how do you get configuration into a container?
invariant: Non-secret configuration is stored as a namespaced object and mounted or injected as env.
answer_30s: ConfigMaps hold non-sensitive key/value configuration, consumed as environment variables, files in a volume, or CLI args.
answer_2m: |
  The subtle behaviour interviewers ask about: env-var consumers never see an
  update without a Pod restart, while volume-mounted ConfigMaps are updated in
  place (with a propagation delay, and only for files, not for subPath mounts).
  That difference decides whether your change requires a rollout or a
  reload/restart hook. Keep secrets in Secrets, not ConfigMaps -- a ConfigMap
  is neither encrypted nor access-controlled beyond normal RBAC.
when_to_use:
  - non-secret settings shared by several workloads
avoid_when:
  - credentials or tokens (use Secret) or values that must change without a restart
commands:
  - kubectl get configmap -A => which configuration objects exist
  - kubectl get configmap api-config -o yaml => what values the running config actually holds
  - kubectl describe pod api-... => which ConfigMap keys the Pod mounted and whether any are missing
failure_modes:
  - required key missing, so the container fails to start
  - expecting env-var changes to apply without a restart
  - putting secrets in a ConfigMap
related:
  - k8s.secret
  - k8s.volume
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/
